A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 24 m/s in 8.0 s. How far does it travel in that time?

{{FIRST_STUB}}
[attached file: stemLM Guide.txt]

```stemlm
@meta
subject: Physics
topic: Distance under constant acceleration
question: A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 24 m/s in 8.0 s. How far does it travel in that time?
@endmeta
@step id=s1
title: Find the acceleration
@formula id=e1
$$a=\frac{v-u}{t}$$
@endformula
@body
The car starts from rest, so $u=0$. Acceleration is the change in velocity each second: $a=\frac{24-0}{8.0}=3.0\ \text{m/s}^2$.
@endbody
@endstep
@step id=s2
title: Use the distance equation for constant acceleration
@formula id=e2
$$s=ut+\tfrac12 at^2$$
@endformula
@body
With $u=0$ the first term vanishes, so $s=\tfrac12(3.0)(8.0)^2=96\ \text{m}$.
@endbody
@takeaway
From rest, distance grows with the square of the time.
@endtakeaway
@endstep
@step id=s3
title: Check with the average velocity
@body
For constant acceleration the average velocity is $\frac{0+24}{2}=12\ \text{m/s}$. Over 8.0 s that gives $12\times 8.0=96\ \text{m}$, the same distance.
@endbody
@endstep
@verify
methods: alt
status: pass
notes: average velocity 12 m/s x 8.0 s = 96 m agrees with s = 96 m
@endverify
@solution
The car travels $96\ \text{m}$ while it speeds up at $3.0\ \text{m/s}^2$.
@endsolution
@end
```

